Having built a foundation of legal restraint during the Convent Burnigns and the Segura crisis, the PSOE leadership successfully whips the party to pass a moderated version of Article 26. State funding for the clergy is phased out in a period of two years but religious orders are permitted to retain some of their educational institutions under state supervision.

Alcalá-Zamora and Maura remain in government! The *Derecha Liberal Republicana* (DLR) survives as a viable, conservative-democratic pillar capable of attracting moderate Catholic voters away from radical anti-republican movements. However, UGT workers and radical leftists accuse the PSOE of "spineless appeasement of the clergy."